{"id":"h079","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"Passengers on a carnival ride move at constant speed in a horizontal circle of radius 5.0 m, making a complete circle in 4.0 s. What is their acceleration? (Unit: m/s^2))\nIs the correct answer \"7.2\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct acceleration is about 12.3 m/s², not 7.2 m/s².","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The passengers undergo uniform circular motion, so the acceleration is centripetal, a = v²/r.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The speed is v = 2πr/T ≈ 7.85 m/s.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Using a = v²/r gives about 12.3 m/s².","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The formula a = 4π²r/T² also gives about 12.3 m/s², consistent with the first method.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Nothing in the problem supports the value 7.2.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Common mistakes (using v/r, wrong period, confusing radius and diameter) do not produce 7.2.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The calculation is direct and unambiguous, so 12.3 m/s² is correct.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"